From 851b09915c31ac53e63ebaab89f527446a603b91 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kirn Gill II <segin2005@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 10 Jun 2026 19:17:01 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 59/98] futex: complete op set, fix timed-wait/EINTR hangs,
 wire NetBSD+OpenBSD

Implement the missing futex ops and fix the lost-wakeup bugs behind the
HexChat (glib g_cond_wait_until) hang, plus a 137-checkpoint torture test.

Ops (sys/kern/futex.c, sys/kern/sleepq.c):
- FUTEX_WAIT_BITSET / FUTEX_WAKE_BITSET: new thread_t.futex_bitset wait
  mask + sleepq_wake_bitset() that wakes only waiters whose mask overlaps;
  plain FUTEX_WAIT registers MATCH_ANY.  WAIT_BITSET honours absolute
  timeouts.
- FUTEX_WAKE_OP: full op/cmp/oparg/cmparg decode (incl. OPARG_SHIFT),
  atomic *uaddr2 update, conditional second wake.

Lost-wakeup / EINTR fixes:
- A timed FUTEX_WAIT that timed out was left linked on the sleepq
  (sched_tick only flips state, never unlinks); a later FUTEX_WAKE on the
  same address consumed the dangling entry and the real waiter blocked for
  its full timeout -- the HexChat hang.  The waiter now self-removes from
  the sleepq on wake and resets sleep_status per wait.
- sys_thr_kill only set sig_pending and never woke a target parked in an
  interruptible sleep, so pthread_kill never delivered EINTR to a futex
  waiter.  Add signal_wake_thread() and call it from thr_kill/thr_kill2;
  the futex WAIT path marks itself THREAD_F_INTERRUPTIBLE and honours EINTR.

Personalities: NetBSD __futex (166, 7-arg, val2 repacked into the timeout
slot) and OpenBSD futex (331, 5-arg, op-number remap) route to sys_futex.

Kernel hardening (the test surfaced this): i386_extract_syscall_args read
useresp[1..8] directly, so a thread whose esp sat within 0x20 of its stack
top made the unconditional 8-word read overrun the mapping and PANIC the
kernel.  Read fault-safely via copyin (bulk fast path + word-by-word
fallback); a bad user esp can no longer panic the kernel.

Test: tests/lib/futex/ (137 checks, 137/137 on Linux; all futex-semantic
sections pass on substrate).  Wired into build-rootfs.sh.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
